actors: Digest job | Lab IdP | This app

step: A job with no human asks for a token
msg: Digest job -> Lab IdP: POST /token, id + secret
text: The nightly digest job wakes up on a schedule. No browser, no login page,
  no consent screen: nobody is there. It does the only thing it can, which is
  to present its own credentials and ask for an access token.

step: The IdP checks who is calling
note: Lab IdP: checks the secret
text: A client id is a name, not a secret. It shows up in logs, config files
  and URLs. The proof is the secret, which the IdP stores only as a hash and
  compares in constant time.

step: The IdP decides what the job may hold
note: Lab IdP: keeps allowed scopes only
text: The job asked for some scopes. The IdP intersects them with the list the
  job was registered with. The job's permissions come from that registration,
  never from what it asks for on the day.

step: The token comes back with no user in it
msg: Lab IdP -> Digest job: access token + stats:read
text: The token's `sub` is the job's own client id, because the job is acting
  as itself. That detail matters in step 7.

step: The job calls the app with the token
msg: Digest job -> This app: GET /internal/stats + token
text: The token travels in an `Authorization: Bearer` header. The app has
  never heard of this job, so everything it learns comes from the token.

step: The app asks the IdP whether to believe it
msg: This app -> Lab IdP: POST /introspect
text: Same call as lab 05, with the app authenticating as a resource server.
  This is also what makes revocation work for services: revoke the token and
  the very next call fails.

step: The IdP describes the token
msg: Lab IdP -> This app: active, sub, scope
text: The answer says the token is active and gives its `sub`, `client_id` and
  `scope`. A user's token and a service's token both look like this. The app
  has to tell them apart itself.

step: The app lets the call through, or doesn't
note: This app: service token + scope?
msg: This app -> Digest job: 200 + the numbers
text: Three checks, in order: the token is active, it was issued to a service
  (not a person), and it holds `stats:read`. Skip the middle one and a
  human's token with the right scope opens a door built for machines.
